1 EDIT
    The EDIT command edits a file with an editor on the host, such as vi.
    govax copies the file to a scratch directory on the host as lines of
    text, runs the editor on the copy, and if the editor wrote it, makes
    what it wrote the next version of the file.

    Syntax:

         EDIT file-spec [/HOST]

    Parameters:

         file-spec    One file.  If no file has the name, and the name has
                      no version, EDIT makes a new one (variable-length
                      records, carriage-return carriage control) if the
                      editor writes it.

    Qualifiers:

         /HOST        The file is a host file, replaced when the editor
                      has written its copy.

    The new version has the record format, record attributes, protection,
    and owner of the version edited, so a line becomes a record of the
    same kind:

         Fixed        A short line is filled out with blanks.  The editor
                      doesn't see the blanks at the end of a record.
         Variable,    The line is the record.
         stream
         VFC          The line is the record, after control bytes: those
                      all the records had, or if they differed, the usual
                      ones for the file, with a warning.

    A line longer than a fixed record, or than a file's maximum record
    size, loses the blanks at its end if that is enough.  Otherwise it is
    the error EDIT-E-TOOLONG, and there is no new version.

    The file is left alone if the editor doesn't write its copy (:q! in
    vi) or ends with a failure status (:cq in vi).  When the editor wrote
    its copy but that can't become a new version, the message ends with
    -EDIT-I-KEPT and the name of the copy, which stays until you delete it.

    A file that isn't lines of text is refused before the editor starts: a
    directory, a relative or indexed file, one with undefined records, or
    one whose records hold a null byte or a line feed, as an image does.
    So is a file on a volume mounted read-only.

    Every process waits while the editor runs.

    See also the help for SET EDITOR and SHOW EDITOR.
